          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My dad is 80, confused since this morning and has not passed water since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what would be looked for?",
          "answer": "**This needs same-day emergency assessment. Please get him seen now, not at a routine appointment.** Call emergency services immediately if he is hard to wake, has trouble breathing, has chest pain, or shows any sign of collapse.\n\nThe triage tool returned \"routine\" because none of its listed patterns matched. That is not a clearance. Several of the guidance notes I reviewed say new confusion needs urgent assessment (the heart failure and common cold notes), and the urinary infection note says confusion can be the main sign of infection in older adults. Confusion together with no urine for a day should be treated as urgent.\n\n**What clinicians would look for:**\n\n- **Urinary infection:** The urinary infection note says that in older adults, confusion or a fall may be the main sign, with few typical urinary symptoms. Fever, chills, or back or side pain would make it more serious.\n- **Blood sugar problems:** The diabetes note says low blood sugar can cause confusion. Very high blood sugar with drowsiness or confusion is also an emergency. If he has diabetes and a meter is available, checking his blood sugar now is a sensible step while you arrange help.\n- **Heart failure:** The heart failure note lists confusion as a reason for urgent assessment.\n- **Other causes:** Urinary retention (being unable to pass urine), kidney injury, salt or fluid imbalance, medication effects, and stroke are all things a clinician would consider. My search for detailed guidance on these failed, so I cannot cite the sources for them.\n\n**What to do now:** Take him to urgent care or the emergency department today. Tell staff exactly when the confusion started and when he last passed urine. Bring a full list of his medicines, including any blood thinners, diabetes medicines, or water tablets.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
